<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple in Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Second Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was the central sanctuary of ancient Jewish worship. After the first temple was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, the Achaemenid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> permitted exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to return and rebuild it, and the Second Temple was completed in the late sixth century BCE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> cite this as an example of Persian tolerance towards subject peoples' religions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Temple in Jerusalem" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Modell_BW_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cyrus Cylinder has been celebrated in modern times as an early articulation of humane rule, and the Achaemenid treatment of the Jewish exiles in Babylonia, allowing the rebuilding of the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b>, had lasting consequences for the development of Judaism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jeroboam ruled first from Shechem and later from Tirzah, and he set up royal sanctuaries at Bethel and Dan, each with a golden calf, so that his subjects need not go on pilgrimage to the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Seleucid confrontation with the Maccabean revolt in Judaea (167–142 BCE), triggered by Antiochus IV Epiphanes's desecration of the <b>Jerusalem Temple</b> and attempted suppression of Jewish religious practice, produced the independent Hasmonean state, commemorated in the festival of Hanukkah, whose political and religious dynamics shaped the world into which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible describes Solomon dividing Israel into twelve administrative districts, each supplying the court for one month a year, and imposing corvée labour for building projects, including the <b>Temple of Jerusalem</b> and his palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…said by Prosper of Aquitaine to have begged Gaiseric to be content with pillage, carried off countless valuables, among them the spoils of the <b>Temple in Jerusalem</b> brought to Rome by Titus, and the empress Licinia Eudoxia and her daughters, one of whom, Eudocia, married Huneric at Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the following centuries, under Persian, Greek and then Roman rule, the scriptures were gathered and edited and the religion of the <b>Second Temple</b> period developed, with its priests, its scribes and its rival schools: the Pharisees, the Sadducees, the Essenes and others.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
